vlsiverify-026:
**Key idea:** An objection is a counter shared by all components and sequences for a time-consuming phase. Activity raises it and completion drops it. When the count returns to 0 (and any drain time expires), the phase ends and UVM moves on to extract, check and report. Without objections, `run_phase` would end at time 0, or would never end if you used `forever` loops everywhere.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "test objection",  "wave": "01.....0..." },
  { "name": "vseq objection",  "wave": "0.1...0...." },
  { "name": "objection count", "wave": "x2=...=2...", "data": ["1","2","1","0"] },
  { "name": "drain time",      "wave": "0......1.0." },
  { "name": "run_phase active","wave": "01.......0." }
],
  "head": { "text": "phase ends only after count reaches 0 and drain time expires" } }
```

### Explanation
```systemverilog
task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  phase.raise_objection(this, "start main traffic");
  vseq.start(env.vsqr);
  phase.drop_objection(this, "main traffic done");
endtask

function void end_of_elaboration_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  // wait 1us after the last drop so in-flight data reaches the scoreboard
  uvm_phase rp = uvm_run_phase::get();
  rp.phase_done.set_drain_time(this, 1us);
endfunction
```
- Drivers and monitors run `forever` loops and do **not** raise objections. The test or top virtual sequence owns them, and in-flight data is covered by drain time or `phase_ready_to_end`.
- The objection hierarchy propagates counts up to `uvm_top`, so `+UVM_OBJECTION_TRACE` shows who is holding the phase.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Raising an objection after delta 0 of a phase that has no other objection is too late: the phase may already have ended. Raise at the very start of the task.
- An objection that is never dropped hangs the simulation until the global timeout (default 9200 s).
- Too many raise/drop calls (per item) slow simulation. Raise per test or sequence instead.

### Follow-ups
- *How do you delay the end without an objection?* Implement `phase_ready_to_end()` and re-raise there while the scoreboard is still waiting.

vlsiverify-037:
**Key idea:** A **monitor** passively samples the virtual interface, rebuilds a new transaction for each protocol operation, and broadcasts it on an analysis port. A **scoreboard** receives expected and actual streams (through imps or analysis FIFOs), predicts or queues the expected items, compares, and reports leftovers in `check_phase`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  VIF["vif (clocking block)"] --> MON["monitor.run_phase<br/>wait valid and ready, sample fields"]
  MON -->|"ap.write(tr)"| SCB
  subgraph SCB["scoreboard"]
    EXP["exp_fifo (from input monitor + ref model)"] --> CMP["compare in run_phase"]
    ACT["act_fifo (from output monitor)"] --> CMP
    CMP --> REP["counts, check_phase leftovers"]
  end
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class my_monitor extends uvm_monitor;
  `uvm_component_utils(my_monitor)
  virtual dut_if            vif;
  uvm_analysis_port #(pkt)  ap;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    ap = new("ap", this);
  endfunction

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual dut_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "vif missing")
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    forever begin
      pkt tr;
      @(vif.mon_cb iff (vif.mon_cb.valid && vif.mon_cb.ready));
      tr = pkt::type_id::create("tr");     // new object per transaction
      tr.addr = vif.mon_cb.addr;
      tr.data = vif.mon_cb.data;
      ap.write(tr);
    end
  endtask
endclass

class my_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard;
  `uvm_component_utils(my_scoreboard)
  uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo #(pkt) exp_fifo, act_fifo;
  int n_match, n_mismatch;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    exp_fifo = new("exp_fifo", this);
    act_fifo = new("act_fifo", this);
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    pkt exp, act;
    forever begin
      act_fifo.get(act);
      exp_fifo.get(exp);
      if (act.compare(exp)) n_match++;
      else begin
        n_mismatch++;
        `uvm_error("SCB", $sformatf("exp %s act %s", exp.convert2string(), act.convert2string()))
      end
    end
  endtask

  function void check_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    if (!exp_fifo.is_empty() || !act_fifo.is_empty())
      `uvm_error("SCB", $sformatf("leftover exp=%0d act=%0d", exp_fifo.used(), act_fifo.used()))
  endfunction

  function void report_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    `uvm_info("SCB", $sformatf("match=%0d mismatch=%0d", n_match, n_mismatch), UVM_NONE)
  endfunction
endclass
// env: in_mon.ap -> refmodel -> scb.exp_fifo.analysis_export ; out_mon.ap -> scb.act_fifo.analysis_export
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Sampling through a clocking block avoids races with the DUT's own clock edge.
- Reset during traffic: flush the FIFOs and expected queues when the monitor sees reset, or you get false mismatches.

### Follow-ups
- *Out-of-order outputs?* Replace the paired FIFO compare with an associative array keyed by ID (see in-order vs out-of-order scoreboards).

vlsiverify-043:
**Key idea:** `uvm_heartbeat` is a liveness watchdog. Registered components must show activity (by raising or dropping a `uvm_callbacks_objection`) within every window defined by a heartbeat event. If they do not, according to the mode (all, one, or any must be active), it reports a fatal error. That catches hangs early instead of waiting for the global timeout.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "hb_event (window)", "wave": "10..10..10..10.." },
  { "name": "drv activity",      "wave": "0101010.......0." },
  { "name": "mon activity",      "wave": "0.10.10..10....." },
  { "name": "HB check",          "wave": "x...2...2...3...", "data": ["ok","ok","FATAL drv"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "UVM_ALL_ACTIVE: drv shows no activity in window 3, so fatal" } }
```

### Explanation
```systemverilog
class my_env extends uvm_env;
  uvm_callbacks_objection hb_obj;
  uvm_heartbeat           hb;
  uvm_event               hb_evt;

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    hb_obj = new("hb_obj");
    hb     = new("hb", this, hb_obj);
    hb_evt = new("hb_evt");
    // also share hb_obj with the driver, e.g. via config_db
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    uvm_component comps[$];
    comps.push_back(agt.drv);
    comps.push_back(agt.mon);
    hb.set_mode(UVM_ALL_ACTIVE);
    hb.set_heartbeat(hb_evt, comps);       // starts monitoring
    forever #10us hb_evt.trigger();        // window definition
  endtask
endclass

// inside the driver, once per item
hb_obj.raise_objection(this);
hb_obj.drop_objection(this);
```
| Mode | Fatal if |
| --- | --- |
| `UVM_ALL_ACTIVE` | Any registered component is silent in a window |
| `UVM_ONE_ACTIVE` | Not exactly one component is active |
| `UVM_ANY_ACTIVE` | No component is active |
| `UVM_NO_HB_MODE` | Never (disabled) |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Stop it (`hb.stop()`) during legitimately idle periods (long reset, low-power sleep), or you get false fatals.
- The objection is a *separate* `uvm_callbacks_objection`, not the phase objection. Activity on it does not keep the phase alive.

### Follow-ups
- *Heartbeat vs timeout?* A timeout catches "test never ended". A heartbeat catches "component stopped making progress", which is faster and points at the culprit.

vlsiverify-044:
**Key idea:** A singleton class allows exactly **one instance**. The constructor is `protected` and a static `get()` creates the instance on first call and returns the same handle afterwards. UVM uses the pattern for global services: `uvm_root` (`uvm_top`), `uvm_factory`, `uvm_report_server`, `uvm_resource_pool`, ...

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  C1["component A: cfg_mgr::get()"] --> G{"m_inst == null?"}
  C2["component B: cfg_mgr::get()"] --> G
  G -->|"yes, first call"| N["m_inst = new()"] --> R["return m_inst"]
  G -->|"no"| R
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class addr_allocator extends uvm_object;
  local static addr_allocator m_inst;      // the one instance
  local bit [31:0] next_free = 'h1000;

  protected function new(string name = "addr_allocator");  // nobody else can call new
    super.new(name);
  endfunction

  static function addr_allocator get();
    if (m_inst == null) m_inst = new();
    return m_inst;
  endfunction

  function bit [31:0] alloc(int unsigned bytes);
    alloc     = next_free;
    next_free += bytes;
  endfunction
endclass

// any sequence, any agent
bit [31:0] base = addr_allocator::get().alloc(256);
```

| UVM singleton | Access |
| --- | --- |
| `uvm_root` | `uvm_root::get()` / `uvm_top` |
| Factory | `uvm_factory::get()` / `uvm_coreservice_t::get().get_factory()` |
| Report server | `uvm_report_server::get_server()` |
| Resource pool | `uvm_resource_pool::get()` |
| Global event/barrier pools | `uvm_event_pool::get_global_pool()` |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Singletons are global state. Results become order-dependent and harder to test, so use them for truly global services only.
- The instance survives for the whole simulation. Reset its state explicitly if a test needs it cleared.

### Follow-ups
- *Why `protected new` instead of `local`?* `protected` still allows a subclass, while blocking `new()` from outside.

artofverif-008:
**Key idea:** `uvm_agent` has a field `is_active` of type `uvm_active_passive_enum` (default `UVM_ACTIVE`). The env or test sets it **before the agent is built** through `uvm_config_db` (the deprecated `set_config_int` was the old way) or through an agent config object. The agent's `build_phase` calls `get_is_active()` and builds the sequencer and driver only when active.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant T as test.build_phase
  participant DB as uvm_config_db
  participant A as agent.build_phase
  T->>DB: set uvm_active_passive_enum (this, "env.out_agt", "is_active", UVM_PASSIVE)
  T->>T: create env (env creates out_agt)
  A->>A: super.build_phase: uvm_agent gets is_active
  A->>A: get_is_active() == UVM_PASSIVE
  A->>A: build monitor only
```

### Explanation
**Option 1: config_db on the built-in field**
```systemverilog
// test or env build_phase, before creating the agent
uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::set(this, "env.out_agt", "is_active", UVM_PASSIVE);

// agent
class my_agent extends uvm_agent;
  `uvm_component_utils(my_agent)
  my_sqr sqr; my_drv drv; my_mon mon;
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);              // uvm_agent reads "is_active" from config_db
    mon = my_mon::type_id::create("mon", this);
    if (get_is_active() == UVM_ACTIVE) begin
      sqr = my_sqr::type_id::create("sqr", this);
      drv = my_drv::type_id::create("drv", this);
    end
  endfunction
  function void connect_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    if (get_is_active() == UVM_ACTIVE) drv.seq_item_port.connect(sqr.seq_item_export);
  endfunction
endclass
```
**Option 2: agent config object (common in VIP)**
```systemverilog
class my_agent_cfg extends uvm_object;
  uvm_active_passive_enum is_active = UVM_ACTIVE;
  virtual my_if vif;
  `uvm_object_utils(my_agent_cfg)
  function new(string name = "my_agent_cfg"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass
// test: cfg.is_active = UVM_PASSIVE; uvm_config_db#(my_agent_cfg)::set(this, "env.out_agt", "cfg", cfg);
// agent: get cfg, then is_active = cfg.is_active;
```

| Method | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `set_config_int("env.agt", "is_active", UVM_PASSIVE)` | Deprecated in UVM 1.2 |
| `uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::set(...)` | Recommended |
| `uvm_config_db#(int)::set(...)` | Also accepted by `uvm_agent` in UVM 1.2 for compatibility |
| Config object field | Recommended for VIP |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Setting it after the agent's `build_phase` has no effect, because the structure is already built.
- The path must match the agent instance itself (`"env.out_agt"`), not `"env.out_agt.*"`.

### Follow-ups
- *Can it change at runtime?* No. Active/passive is structural and fixed at build time.

artofverif-010:
**Key idea:** There are three steps. **Create** the sequence through the factory, **configure or randomize** its knobs, then **start** it on a sequencer with the blocking `start()`. Typically you raise an objection around the start.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["1. create<br/>seq = my_seq::type_id::create('seq')"] --> B["2. configure / randomize<br/>seq.randomize() with {num == 20}"]
  B --> C["3. start<br/>seq.start(env.agt.sqr)"]
  C --> D["start() returns after body() completes"]
```

### Solution
```systemverilog
class my_test extends base_test;
  `uvm_component_utils(my_test)
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    my_seq seq;
    // 1) create via factory (overridable)
    seq = my_seq::type_id::create("seq");
    // 2) configure / randomize knobs
    if (!seq.randomize() with { num_pkts inside {[10:20]}; })
      `uvm_fatal("RND", "sequence randomize failed")
    seq.cfg = env.cfg;                       // plain assignments also fine
    // 3) start on the target sequencer (blocking)
    phase.raise_objection(this);
    seq.start(env.agt.sqr);
    phase.drop_objection(this);
  endtask
endclass
```

| Step | Alternatives |
| --- | --- |
| Create | `` `uvm_create(seq) `` inside a parent sequence |
| Configure | `uvm_config_db` read inside the sequence (`get(null, get_full_name(), ...)`) |
| Start | `` `uvm_do_on(seq, sqr) `` from a parent, or `default_sequence` through config_db |

```systemverilog
// default_sequence alternative (UVM 1.2)
uvm_config_db#(uvm_object_wrapper)::set(this, "env.agt.sqr.main_phase",
                                        "default_sequence", my_seq::type_id::get());
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without an objection, `run_phase` may end before the sequence sends anything.
- Starting on a null or wrong sequencer gives fatal errors in `start_item`. Starting on a sequencer of a different item type fails the `$cast` in `start_item`.

### Follow-ups
- *Running two sequences concurrently?* Fork the `start()` calls. The sequencer arbitrates between them.
